- How would you reconstruct all k blocks from the backpointers?
- How might you optimize to O(nk) space or prune inner loop with bit tricks?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a fresh DP angle: interval-based value depends on distinct characters within each substring, plus precise reconstruction for k blocks.

## Key Concepts

- Non-overlapping interval DP
- Precomputing distinct counts for all substrings
- Reconstruction via back-pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
function maxKSubstrings(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dist = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    const freq = new Array(26).fill(0);
    let setBits = 0;
    for (let j = i; j >= 0; j--) {
      const idx = s.charCodeAt(j) - 97;
      if (freq[idx]++ === 0) setBits++;
      dist[j][i] = setBits;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  const start = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i][0] = 0;
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      // Option 1: skip i
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
        prev[i][t] = i-1;
        start[i][t] = start[i-1][t];
      }

      // Option 2: end t-th substring at i
      for (let s = 1; s <= i; s++) {
        const val = dp[s-1][t-1] + dist[s-1][i-1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = s-1;
          start[i][t] = s-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct substrings
  const result = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    if (prev[i][t] === i-1) {
      i--;
    } else {
      result.unshift([start[i][t]+1, i]);
      i = prev[i][t];
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], substrings: result };
}
```

Time complexity: O(n²k) for DP transitions + O(n²) for preprocessing. Space: O(n² + nk). Optimizations include limiting the inner loop or using convex hull tricks for special cases.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests translating a DP state into a practical recurrence with non-overlap constraint and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- DP over index i and count t
- Non-overlapping constraint handled by dp[i-2] for taking a domino
- Backtracking to recover indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function solve(A, k){
  const n=A.length;
  const NEG=-1e15;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for(let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0]=0;
  for(let i=2;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      const v1=dp[i-1][t];
      const v2=dp[i-2][t-1]+A[i-2]+A[i-1];
      if(v2>v1){ dp[i][t]=v2; take[i][t]=true; }
      else dp[i][t]=v1;
    }
  }
  // backtrack
  // ... reconstruct domino starts from i=n, t=k
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to optimize to O(nk) time and O(k) space?
- How to adapt if dominoes have variable lengths or weights?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests advanced dynamic programming techniques involving multiple non-overlapping segments with per-element costs—a pattern frequently appearing in interviews for algorithm-intensive roles.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP with fixed number of blocks requiring optimal segmentation
- Prefix sum transformations to simplify recurrence relations
- Time-space optimization strategies and solution reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValue(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const P = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) P[i+1] = P[i] + A[i];

  const B = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let x = 0; x <= n; x++) B[x] = P[x] - c * x;

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i][t-1];
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        const candidate = dp[j][t-1] + B[i] - B[j];
        if (candidate > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = candidate;
          parent[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct solution
  const blocks = [];
  let curr = n, currT = k;
  while (currT > 0 && parent[curr][currT] !== -1) {
    const start = parent[curr][currT];
    blocks.unshift([start, curr-1]);
    curr = start;
    currT--;
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Implementation Details
- The transformation B[x] = P[x] - c*x captures the net value after maintenance costs
- The nested loop structure ensures we consider all possible segment boundaries
- Parent array enables O(k) reconstruction of optimal segment endpoints

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a novel fixed-gap non-crossing pairing constraint, a natural extension of classic non-crossing matching problems, while probing DP state design and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes with fixed pair distance
- Non-crossing/disjoint constraint handling
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Space/time optimization trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxFixedGapPairs(A, d, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(false));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
      if (t > 0 && i - d - 1 >= 0) {
        const cand = dp[i - d - 1][t - 1] + A[i - 1] * A[i - d - 1];
        if (cand > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = cand;
          take[i][t] = true;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  const pairs = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    if (take[i][t]) {
      pairs.push([i - d, i]); // 1-based indices of the pair
      i = i - d - 1;
      t--;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }
  pairs.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], pairs };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model budgeted multi-interval optimization where a fixed daily cost incentivizes shorter campaigns. Requires stateful DP with reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- Up to k non-overlapping subarrays
- Per-element cost transformation: B[i] = A[i] - c
- DP states: end[i][t], best[i][t]
- Recurrences: end[i][t] = max(end[i-1][t] + B[i], best[i-1][t-1] + B[i]); best[i][t] = max(best[i-1][t], end[i][t])
- Reconstruction via backpointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarraysWithCost(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const B = A.map(x => x - c);
  const end = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const best = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prevEnd = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(null));

  best[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    end[i][0] = -Infinity;
    best[i][0] = 0;
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const extend = end[i-1][t] + B[i-1];
      const startNew = best[i-1][t-1] + B[i-1];
      end[i][t] = Math.max(extend, startNew);
      if (best[i-1][t] >= end[i][t]) {
        best[i][t] = best[i-1][t];
      } else {
        best[i][t] = end[i][t];
        prevEnd[i][t] = true;
      }
    }
  }

  let bestVal = -Infinity, bestT = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
    if (best[n][t] > bestVal) { bestVal = best[n][t]; bestT = t; }
  }
  return {profit: bestVal, segments: []};
}
```

